    "leafs": {
      "full_name": "Toronto Maple Leafs",
      "league": "NHL",
      "record": "32-36-14",
      "detail": "8th in Atlantic Division",
      "lotl": {
        "label": "What's Going On With the Leafs",
        "body": "The spring ended the way too many Maple Leaf springs do - with the club left staring at its own reflection and finding too much familiar disappointment. <strong>Toronto's season is over at 32-36-14, 8th in the Atlantic Division, a flat, weary finish that left it with 78 points and a 31-game gap to the front.</strong> That is not a club on the cusp. That is a club that ran out of answers, ran out of purpose, and spent too many nights chasing the game instead of owning it. The L7 streak says it all. This was a team that could not string together the sort of hardened stretch required to matter in this league, and now the hard work begins where it always does in Toronto - in the mirror, not on the ice. Management has decisions to make about the bench, the roster spine, and which veterans still fit a team that needs more backbone than sentiment. <strong>Next comes the ugly but necessary offseason audit: coaching, cap room, and whether this core still deserves another run together.</strong>",
        "updated": "Updated May 23, 2026"
      },
