    "jays": {
      "full_name": "Toronto Blue Jays",
      "league": "MLB",
      "record": "24-27",
      "detail": "3rd in AL East",
      "lotl": {
        "label": "What's Going On With the Jays",
        "body": "Three straight wins have the Blue Jays breathing again, and for the first time in a while the air feels less like April panic and more like a club trying to claw its way back into the race. <strong>Last night's 6-2 thumping of the Pirates completed a tidy sweep in all but name and gave Toronto its third straight victory</strong> after taking two from the Yankees by 2-0 and 2-1 margins. That's the kind of run that steadies a season, especially for a team sitting at 24-27, third in the AL East, still 11 games back and perched on the outside edge of the picture. The Blue Jays have not become a juggernaut overnight - they're still carrying a minus-1.5 run profile and a playoff seed of 9 - but they have at least stopped bleeding. The bats are doing enough, the pitching has smothered opponents for three nights, and the pressure has eased on a roster that needed a pulse. <strong>What matters now is whether they can turn this burst into something durable when they face the Pirates again this afternoon at 2:07 PM ET</strong>.",
        "updated": "Updated May 23, 2026"
      },
