    "commanders": {
      "full_name": "Washington Commanders",
      "league": "NFL",
      "record": "5-12",
      "detail": "3rd in NFC East",
      "lotl": {
        "label": "What's Going On With the Commanders",
        "body": "The draft board has been cleared, the cards are folded away, and now comes the real work - the quiet, unglamorous sort that decides whether a franchise is building something sturdy or just collecting names. Washington sits 3rd in the NFC East, 6 games back, with a W1 streak and the No. 14 seed attached to it like a label on a moving box, but the standings are only the frame now. The picture is the rookie class. <strong>Washington's most important move was stocking the roster with young bodies who can fight for snaps immediately, because this team does not have the luxury of waiting on \"development\" like it's a promise written in pencil.</strong> The Commanders need help where every good team needs it - up front, in the back end, and at receiver - so the pressure falls on the first-year players to make the veterans sweat in camp. That is how depth charts get shaken loose. That is how a post-draft spring becomes something more than a press release. <strong>What matters next is simple: which rookies force their way into the rotation by June, and which ones learn how unforgiving the league is before the pads even come on.</strong>",
        "updated": "Updated May 23, 2026"
      },
